    "title": "States Respond to Supreme Court Ruling on Voting Districts",
    "text": "• The Supreme Court ruling limits the use of race when drawing voting districts, weakening a Civil Rights-era law.\n• Florida's Republican-led Legislature approved new U.S. House districts that could help the GOP win additional seats.\n• Florida Governor Ron DeSantis unveiled a new U.S. House map that redraws a southeastern Florida district.\n• A Florida constitutional amendment prohibits districts from being drawn to deny or diminish the ability of racial or language minorities to elect representatives.\n• Mississippi Governor Tate Reeves announced a special legislative session to redraw voting districts for the state Supreme Court.\n• A federal judge ordered Mississippi to redraw its Supreme Court voting districts after finding they violated the Voting Rights Act.\n• Louisiana lawmakers delayed primaries to respond to a Supreme Court decision on the state's congressional districts.\n• Alabama is under a court order to use a new map until after the next census in 2030.\n• Alabama Attorney General Steve Marshall will apply the ruling to Alabama’s redistricting efforts.\n• Tennessee's Republican U.S. Sen. Marsha Blackburn said the legislature should redraw Tennessee’s only Democratic congressional district.\n• Tennessee's Senate Speaker Randy McNally said redistricting at this time would present logistical challenges.\n• The Illinois House approved a proposed amendment to the state constitution adding protections for race-based redistricting.\n• The Illinois Senate President said the amendment won't pass this session.",
